description:
persona/src/main.rs:282-284:

    /// Fetches a JWT-SVID with the challenge embedded in the audience string, proving
    /// identity to a peer. The challenge becomes part of the JWT claims and signature,
    /// preventing replay attacks.

The mechanism is persona/src/main.rs:326: `format!("{audience}?challenge={challenge}")`, sent as the audience. What actually happens to it:
- persona-grpc/src/service.rs:113 parses that string with AudienceExtensions::parse, which strips only `persona_`-prefixed params (persona-core/src/audience.rs:60-69), so `challenge=` survives into the audience URL and does end up inside the signed `aud` claim.
- Nothing else touches it. The daemon does not know it is a challenge, does not check freshness, uniqueness, or format, and there is no verifier in this codebase that extracts it. Attestor::prove() — the trait method built for exactly this (persona-attestors/src/lib.rs:117-129, which takes a `challenge: &[u8]` described as 'a freshly-generated nonce') — is never invoked on this path at all.

What goes wrong: 'preventing replay attacks' is an unqualified security guarantee in a doc comment. It holds only if the relying party independently parses the challenge back out of the aud claim and checks it against what it issued — an obligation stated nowhere. A reader of this comment reasonably concludes the daemon handles it, and ships a verifier that just checks the signature.

Fix: state what the command does and where the obligation sits — e.g. 'the challenge is carried verbatim in the audience string and therefore covered by the signature; the relying party MUST parse it back out of the aud claim and match it against the nonce it issued. The daemon does not validate the challenge.' Also worth noting for the same comment: line 326 appends '?' unconditionally, so an audience that already has a query string produces two '?'.
